WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
A web application can be vulnerable because of configuration even when the application logic is sound.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:16.580
Secure defaults should disable debug behavior, avoid verbose error exposure, enforce HTTPS, restrict administrative interfaces, and keep sensitive settings out of client-visible responses.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:26.800
Error handling should help operators diagnose issues without showing stack traces, secrets, internal paths, SQL fragments, or framework details to ordinary users.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.340
Logs should be useful, but they should not become a second copy of sensitive data.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.920
Security headers and TLS are important operating controls.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:46.580
Headers such as Content Security Policy, Strict-Transport-Security, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and framing controls can reduce browser-side risk when configured carefully for the application.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.240
TLS protects data in transit and supports user trust, but it must be deployed consistently.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:01:00.060
Mixed content, expired certificates, weak redirects, and insecure internal callbacks can weaken the protection users expect.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.440
Dependencies and deployment platforms are part of application security.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:11.220
Third-party packages can contain known vulnerabilities, malicious updates, weak defaults, or transitive risk.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:19.440
Dependency management should include inventory, version tracking, updates, review of high-risk packages, and removal of unused libraries.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:27.900
Cloud and container environments add permissions, images, secrets, network rules, storage policies, and runtime configuration.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:36.920
Treat environment variables, CI/CD credentials, container registries, and infrastructure templates as security-sensitive application assets.
